arXiv:2603.08429v1 [cs.CL] 09 Mar 2026</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">One Model Is Enough: Native Retrieval Embeddings from LLM Agent Hidden States</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Bo Jiang
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Temple University
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">bo.jiang@temple.edu</span></a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">LLM agents that retrieve external knowledge typically generate a search query as text, then run a separate embedding model to encode it into a vector. This two-model pipeline adds infrastructure complexity and latency, yet is redundant: the LLM already encodes the full conversational context in its hidden states. We propose equipping LLM agents with native retrieval capability by adding a lightweight projection head that maps hidden states directly into the embedding space, eliminating the need for a separate embedding model. Trained with a combination of alignment, contrastive, and rank distillation losses, our method retains 97% of baseline retrieval quality while enabling the LLM agent to search with its own representations. Experiments on the QReCC conversational search benchmark show competitive Recall@10 and MRR@10 compared to the standard generate-then-encode pipeline, with systematic ablations confirming the contribution of each loss component.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para ltx_align_bottom">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center" style="width:345.0pt;"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;"></span></p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Retrieval-augmented generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lewis et al., 2020</a>, RAG;)</cite> has become the standard approach for grounding large language model (LLM) agents in external knowledge. When an LLM agent determines that it needs to search, the typical pipeline operates in two stages: the LLM first generates a search query as natural language text, and then a separate embedding model encodes that text into a dense vector for retrieval against a document index.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This two-model architecture is the dominant paradigm, yet it introduces a fundamental redundancy. The LLM has already processed the full conversational context—user intent, dialogue history, and task requirements—and encoded this understanding in its hidden states. The generated query text is merely a lossy, discrete projection of this rich internal representation. A second model must then re-process this text from scratch, recovering semantic information that the LLM already possessed. In effect, the pipeline discards the LLM’s internal understanding only to approximate it again with a separate encoder.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We argue that this redundancy is unnecessary. If the LLM’s hidden states already capture the information needed for retrieval, a lightweight learned projection should be able to map them directly into the embedding space—giving the LLM agent a <em id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">native</em> retrieval capability without requiring a second model.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We propose a simple approach: attach a small projection head to the LLM that transforms its hidden states into vectors compatible with an existing embedding space. The projection head is trained via knowledge distillation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hinton et al., 2015</a>)</cite> from the embedding model using three complementary objectives: (1) an <em id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">alignment loss</em> that minimizes the distance between projected vectors and teacher embeddings, (2) a <em id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">contrastive loss</em> that preserves the relative structure among queries, and (3) a <em id="S1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">rank distillation loss</em> that transfers the teacher’s document ranking preferences. Once trained, the embedding model is no longer needed at inference time—the LLM agent searches using its own representations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate our method on the QReCC conversational search benchmark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anantha et al., 2021</a>)</cite> in a same-family setting (Qwen3-8B as the LLM agent, Qwen3-Embedding-8B as the teacher). Our best configuration retains 97% of the baseline’s retrieval quality while completely eliminating the embedding model from the inference pipeline. Systematic ablations across 12 configurations confirm that all three loss components contribute, and that extended training with lower learning rates is the most effective recipe.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Our contributions are:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We identify and formalize the redundancy in the standard two-model retrieval pipeline for LLM agents, and propose native retrieval via hidden state projection as an alternative.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We design a three-loss training objective combining alignment, contrastive, and rank distillation losses for projecting LLM hidden states into an embedding space.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We provide comprehensive experiments with 12 ablation configurations, bootstrap confidence intervals, and statistical significance tests, demonstrating near-parity retrieval quality without a separate embedding model.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Retrieval-Augmented Generation.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">RAG systems ground LLM outputs in retrieved evidence to reduce hallucination and improve factuality <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lewis et al., 2020</a>)</cite>. The standard architecture uses a retrieve-then-generate pipeline where a query is encoded by a dedicated embedding model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karpukhin et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Reimers and Gurevych, 2019</a>)</cite> and matched against a pre-indexed document collection. Conversational RAG extends this to multi-turn settings, where query understanding requires resolving coreferences and topic shifts across dialogue history. Our work targets this pipeline but questions the necessity of the dedicated embedding model.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Conversational Query Rewriting.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In multi-turn retrieval, raw user utterances are often ambiguous without context <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anantha et al., 2021</a>)</cite>. Query rewriting approaches use the LLM to generate a self-contained search query that incorporates dialogue history. While effective, this produces text that must still be encoded by a separate embedding model. Our approach bypasses this encode step entirely by projecting the LLM’s hidden states—which already reflect the full conversational context—directly into the embedding space.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">LLM-Based Embeddings.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recent work has explored using LLMs themselves as embedding models. LLM2Vec <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">BehnamGhader et al., 2024</a>)</cite> modifies pretrained LLMs with bidirectional attention and contrastive training to produce general-purpose text embeddings. NV-Embed <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al., 2024</a>)</cite> applies latent attention pooling over LLM hidden states. These methods repurpose the LLM as a <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">replacement</em> embedding model, requiring a separate forward pass in embedding mode. In contrast, our approach operates on hidden states that are <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">already computed</em> during the LLM’s normal generative inference, adding only a lightweight projection head rather than a full additional forward pass.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Knowledge Distillation for Retrieval.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Knowledge distillation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hinton et al., 2015</a>)</cite> has been widely applied to compress retrieval models, typically by training a smaller student encoder to mimic a larger teacher’s representations or rankings. Rank distillation specifically transfers document ranking preferences rather than point-wise embeddings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hofstätter et al., 2021</a>)</cite>. Our setting differs in that the student is not a standalone encoder but a projection head attached to an LLM, and the goal is not compression but <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">elimination</em> of the encoder entirely.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Hypothetical Document Embeddings.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">HyDE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al., 2022</a>)</cite> generates a hypothetical document in response to a query and embeds that document for retrieval, improving query-document matching. While creative, this approach increases generation cost and still requires an embedding model. Our method moves in the opposite direction: rather than generating more text to embed, we avoid text generation for retrieval purposes altogether.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Method</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Problem Setting</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider an LLM agent engaged in multi-turn conversation that periodically needs to retrieve external documents. In the standard pipeline, the agent (1) generates a search query <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> as text tokens <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{1},t_{2},\ldots,t_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{1},t_{2},\ldots,t_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> via autoregressive decoding, then (2) encodes <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> with a separate embedding model <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> to obtain a dense vector <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}=E(q)\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}=E(q)\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> for retrieval against a document index.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">During step (1), the LLM produces hidden states <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}_{1},\mathbf{h}_{2},\ldots,\mathbf{h}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}_{1},\mathbf{h}_{2},\ldots,\mathbf{h}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> as a byproduct of generation, where <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{h}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{h}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the last-layer hidden state at the <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th generated token. These hidden states encode the full conversational context but are discarded. Our goal is to learn a projection function <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\mathbf{h}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{h}_{n})\approx E(q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\mathbf{h}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{h}_{n})\approx E(q)</annotation></semantics></math>, eliminating the need for <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> at inference time.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Hidden State Extraction</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We extract hidden states from the LLM’s normal autoregressive generation process. At each decoding step <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, the LLM computes a forward pass (reusing the KV cache from previous steps) and produces the next token <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> along with its last-layer hidden state <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. We collect <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for all non-special tokens (excluding BOS, EOS, and padding), forming the hidden state sequence <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}=[\mathbf{h}_{1};\mathbf{h}_{2};\ldots;\mathbf{h}_{n}]\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d_{h}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐇</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}=[\mathbf{h}_{1};\mathbf{h}_{2};\ldots;\mathbf{h}_{n}]\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d_{h}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This extraction adds negligible overhead since the hidden states are already computed during generation—we simply retain them rather than discarding them.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Projection Head Architecture</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The projection head <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> transforms the variable-length hidden state sequence <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐇</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}</annotation></semantics></math> into a fixed-dimensional embedding vector. It consists of four components:</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Input Projection.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A linear layer maps from the LLM’s hidden dimension to the mapper’s internal dimension: <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}=\mathbf{H}\mathbf{W}_{\text{in}}+\mathbf{b}_{\text{in}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐇𝐖</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>𝐛</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}=\mathbf{H}\mathbf{W}_{\text{in}}+\mathbf{b}_{\text{in}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{\text{in}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{h}\times d_{m}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{\text{in}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{h}\times d_{m}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Transformer Encoder.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Learnable positional embeddings are added to <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is then processed by a stack of <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> transformer encoder layers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vaswani et al., 2017</a>)</cite> with <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional representations. The self-attention mechanism allows the model to aggregate information across the full generated sequence, capturing dependencies between tokens.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Pooling.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The encoded sequence is compressed into a single vector. We use mean pooling over non-padding positions:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\mathbf{x}_{i}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\mathbf{x}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are the transformer outputs at valid (non-padding) positions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Output Projection and Normalization.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A final linear layer projects to the target embedding dimension, followed by L2 normalization:</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\mathbf{H})=\frac{\mathbf{p}\mathbf{W}_{\text{out}}+\mathbf{b}_{\text{out}}}{\|\mathbf{p}\mathbf{W}_{\text{out}}+\mathbf{b}_{\text{out}}\|_{2}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐇</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>𝐩𝐖</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>𝐛</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub></mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐩𝐖</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>𝐛</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\mathbf{H})=\frac{\mathbf{p}\mathbf{W}_{\text{out}}+\mathbf{b}_{\text{out}}}{\|\mathbf{p}\mathbf{W}_{\text{out}}+\mathbf{b}_{\text{out}}\|_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{\text{out}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{m}\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{\text{out}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{m}\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is the target embedding space dimension. The L2 normalization ensures that retrieval via dot product is equivalent to cosine similarity, matching the embedding model’s expected similarity metric.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>Training Objectives</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We train the projection head via knowledge distillation from the embedding model (the <em id="S3.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">teacher</em>). Given a training set of query hidden state sequences <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}^{(j)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐇</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}^{(j)}</annotation></semantics></math> paired with teacher embeddings <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}^{(j)}=E(q^{(j)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}^{(j)}=E(q^{(j)})</annotation></semantics></math>, we optimize a combination of three losses.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Alignment Loss.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Directly minimizes the angular distance between predicted and teacher embeddings:</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{align}}=1-\frac{1}{B}\sum_{j=1}^{B}\cos\!\big(f(\mathbf{H}^{(j)}),\;\mathbf{y}^{(j)}\big)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>align</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>B</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>B</mi></munderover><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.216em;" width="1.216em"><mi>cos</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐇</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{align}}=1-\frac{1}{B}\sum_{j=1}^{B}\cos\!\big(f(\mathbf{H}^{(j)}),\;\mathbf{y}^{(j)}\big)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is the batch size. This provides a direct training signal toward the teacher’s embedding for each query.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Contrastive Loss.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An InfoNCE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">van den Oord et al., 2018</a>)</cite> loss that encourages the projected embeddings to preserve discriminative structure across queries within each batch:</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{contra}}=-\frac{1}{B}\sum_{j=1}^{B}\log\frac{\exp\!\big(f(\mathbf{H}^{(j)})\cdot\mathbf{y}^{(j)}/\tau\big)}{\sum_{k=1}^{B}\exp\!\big(f(\mathbf{H}^{(j)})\cdot\mathbf{y}^{(k)}/\tau\big)}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>contra</mtext></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>B</mi></mfrac><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>B</mi></msup><mi>log</mi><mfrac><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.370em;" width="1.370em"><mi>exp</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐇</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>B</mi></msubsup><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.370em;" width="1.370em"><mi>exp</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐇</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{contra}}=-\frac{1}{B}\sum_{j=1}^{B}\log\frac{\exp\!\big(f(\mathbf{H}^{(j)})\cdot\mathbf{y}^{(j)}/\tau\big)}{\sum_{k=1}^{B}\exp\!\big(f(\mathbf{H}^{(j)})\cdot\mathbf{y}^{(k)}/\tau\big)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> is a temperature hyperparameter. Each predicted embedding should be most similar to its own teacher embedding relative to other teacher embeddings in the batch.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Rank Distillation Loss.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Transfers the teacher’s document ranking preferences via KL divergence. For each query, we retrieve the teacher’s top-<math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> candidate documents <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbf{d}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{d}_{K}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝐝</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐝</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{d}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{d}_{K}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and their teacher similarity scores <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{k}=\mathbf{y}^{(j)}\cdot\mathbf{d}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>𝐝</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{k}=\mathbf{y}^{(j)}\cdot\mathbf{d}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. The loss aligns the student’s ranking distribution with the teacher’s:</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{rank}}=\text{KL}\!\Big(\text{softmax}\!\big(\mathbf{s}/\tau_{r}\big)\;\Big\|\;\text{softmax}\!\big(\hat{\mathbf{s}}/\tau_{r}\big)\Big)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>rank</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mpadded style="width:1.431em;" width="1.431em"><mtext>KL</mtext></mpadded><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mpadded style="width:3.720em;" width="3.720em"><mtext>softmax</mtext></mpadded><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>𝐬</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="1.600em" rspace="0.447em">∥</mo><mpadded style="width:3.720em;" width="3.720em"><mtext>softmax</mtext></mpadded><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐬</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{rank}}=\text{KL}\!\Big(\text{softmax}\!\big(\mathbf{s}/\tau_{r}\big)\;\Big\|\;\text{softmax}\!\big(\hat{\mathbf{s}}/\tau_{r}\big)\Big)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{s}_{k}=f(\mathbf{H}^{(j)})\cdot\mathbf{d}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐇</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>𝐝</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{s}_{k}=f(\mathbf{H}^{(j)})\cdot\mathbf{d}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> are the student’s scores and <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> is the rank distillation temperature.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Combined Objective.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The total training loss is:</p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}=\lambda_{a}\mathcal{L}_{\text{align}}+\lambda_{c}\mathcal{L}_{\text{contra}}+\lambda_{r}\mathcal{L}_{\text{rank}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>align</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>contra</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>rank</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}=\lambda_{a}\mathcal{L}_{\text{align}}+\lambda_{c}\mathcal{L}_{\text{contra}}+\lambda_{r}\mathcal{L}_{\text{rank}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{a}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{c}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> control the relative contribution of each component. We optimize with AdamW <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Loshchilov and Hutter, 2019</a>)</cite> and cosine learning rate scheduling.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experimental Setup</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Dataset</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate on QReCC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anantha et al., 2021</a>, Question Rewriting in Conversational Context;)</cite>, a large-scale conversational search dataset containing multi-turn dialogues paired with manually rewritten queries and relevant document annotations. We use the <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">qrecc_eval_large</span> split comprising 346 conversations with 2,189 retrieval triggers. The data is partitioned into train, validation, and test splits at the conversation level to prevent information leakage across turns of the same dialogue.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Models</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We adopt a <em id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">same-family</em> setting using Qwen3-8B as the LLM agent and Qwen3-Embedding-8B as the teacher embedding model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qwen Team, 2024</a>)</cite>. This pairing is motivated by the practical scenario where an organization deploys a single model family: the LLM handles generation while its embedding sibling handles retrieval. The hidden state dimension is <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{h}=4096" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>4096</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{h}=4096</annotation></semantics></math> and the target embedding dimension is <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=1024" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=1024</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Baselines</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our baseline is the standard <em id="S4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">generate-then-encode</em> pipeline: the LLM generates a rewritten search query via greedy decoding (up to 32 tokens), and the embedding model encodes it into a dense vector. Documents are retrieved via cosine similarity against a pre-built index. This represents the conventional two-model approach that our method aims to replace.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Projection Head Configuration</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The projection head uses <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{m}=1024" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{m}=1024</annotation></semantics></math> internal dimension, <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=2</annotation></semantics></math> transformer encoder layers with 8 attention heads, and mean pooling. No intermediate projector is used. The total parameter count is approximately 25M—negligible compared to the 8B-parameter LLM.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.5 </span>Training Details</h3>

<div id="S4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The best configuration uses 80 training epochs with a learning rate of <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>, cosine annealing to <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 10^{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 10^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math>, batch size 16, AdamW with weight decay <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>, and gradient clipping at norm 1.0. Loss weights are <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{a}=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{a}=0.5</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{c}=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{c}=0.5</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{r}=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{r}=0.5</annotation></semantics></math> with temperatures <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=\tau_{r}=0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=\tau_{r}=0.05</annotation></semantics></math>. For rank distillation, we use the teacher’s top-<math id="S4.SS5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>128</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=128</annotation></semantics></math> candidate documents.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Training data is generated by running the LLM on training conversations and caching the resulting hidden state traces. This allows rapid experimentation: the initial trace generation takes approximately 2 hours on an NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000, but subsequent mapper training runs complete in 15–20 minutes using cached traces.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.6 </span>Evaluation Metrics</h3>

<div id="S4.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We report Recall@10, MRR@10 (Mean Reciprocal Rank), and nDCG@10 (normalized Discounted Cumulative Gain), computed over the test split. Statistical significance is assessed via McNemar’s test on per-trigger retrieval success, and 95% bootstrap confidence intervals are computed with 1,000 resamples. We also report inference latency (p50) measured on the same GPU.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Results</h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Main Results</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 5.1 Main Results ‣ 5 Results ‣ One Model Is Enough: Native Retrieval Embeddings from LLM Agent Hidden States" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> compares our best projection head configuration against the standard generate-then-encode baseline. Our method retains 95–97% of baseline retrieval quality across all metrics while completely eliminating the embedding model from the inference pipeline.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T1" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S5.T1.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S5.T1.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T1.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"></th>
<th id="S5.T1.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T1.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Recall</span></th>
<th id="S5.T1.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T1.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MRR</span></th>
<th id="S5.T1.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T1.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">nDCG</span></th>
<th id="S5.T1.2.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T1.2.1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Latency</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S5.T1.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T1.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_th ltx_th_row"></th>
<td id="S5.T1.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S5.T1.2.2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">@10</span></td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S5.T1.2.2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">@10</span></td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S5.T1.2.2.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">@10</span></td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S5.T1.2.2.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(p50)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T1.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T1.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">Baseline</th>
<th id="S5.T1.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t">0.637</th>
<th id="S5.T1.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t">0.329</th>
<th id="S5.T1.2.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t">0.402</th>
<th id="S5.T1.2.3.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t">43.5ms</th></tr>
<tr id="S5.T1.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T1.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Ours</th>
<td id="S5.T1.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.607</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.293</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.367</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.4.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">2.0ms</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T1.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T1.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_t"><math id="S5.T1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S5.T1.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t">–3.0%</th>
<th id="S5.T1.2.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t">–3.6%</th>
<th id="S5.T1.2.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t">–3.5%</th>
<th id="S5.T1.2.5.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t">21.8<math id="S5.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math></th></tr>
<tr id="S5.T1.2.6" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T1.2.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb">95% CI</th>
<td id="S5.T1.2.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">[–4.7, –1.4]</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">[–4.7, –2.4]</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.6.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">[–4.5, –2.4]</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.6.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">—</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Main results on QReCC test set. Our projection head achieves near-parity retrieval quality while eliminating the embedding model. Latency is measured per query on an NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000. McNemar’s test: <math id="S5.T1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{2}=12.21" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>12.21</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{2}=12.21</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.T1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.0005" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.0005</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.0005</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The latency reduction from 43.5ms to 2.0ms (21.8<math id="S5.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>) reflects the removal of the embedding model forward pass. The projection head adds only a lightweight matrix multiplication over the already-computed hidden states. McNemar’s test confirms the quality gap is statistically significant (<math id="S5.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.0005" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.0005</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.0005</annotation></semantics></math>), with a per-trigger win/tie/loss breakdown of 140/1843/206—indicating that the two methods agree on 84.2% of triggers.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Loss Ablation</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To understand the contribution of each loss component, we conduct a systematic ablation (Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 5.2 Loss Ablation ‣ 5 Results ‣ One Model Is Enough: Native Retrieval Embeddings from LLM Agent Hidden States" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). All ablation experiments use 30 epochs with learning rate <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>; only the best configuration uses the extended 80-epoch recipe.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T2" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S5.T2.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S5.T2.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T2.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T2.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Configuration</span></th>
<td id="S5.T2.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><math id="S5.T2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> / <math id="S5.T2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> / <math id="S5.T2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T2.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">R@10</span></td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T2.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MRR</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T2.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T2.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">Align only</th>
<td id="S5.T2.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">1.0 / 0.0 / 0.0</td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">0.567</td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">0.273</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T2.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T2.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Contrastive only</th>
<td id="S5.T2.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.0 / 1.0 / 0.0</td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.498</td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.231</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T2.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T2.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Rank distill only</th>
<td id="S5.T2.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.0 / 0.0 / 1.0</td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.001</td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.000</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T2.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T2.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">Align + Contra</th>
<td id="S5.T2.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">0.5 / 0.5 / 0.0</td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">0.582</td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">0.281</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T2.2.6" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T2.2.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Align + Rank</th>
<td id="S5.T2.2.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.5 / 0.0 / 0.5</td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.579</td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.6.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.272</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T2.2.7" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T2.2.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">All three</th>
<td id="S5.T2.2.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.5 / 0.5 / 0.5</td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.7.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.595</td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.7.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.288</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T2.2.8" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T2.2.8.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t">All three + recipe</th>
<td id="S5.T2.2.8.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t">0.5 / 0.5 / 0.5</td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.8.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.T2.2.8.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.607</span></td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.8.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.T2.2.8.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.293</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>Loss ablation results. <math id="S5.T2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{a}</annotation></semantics></math>: alignment, <math id="S5.T2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{c}</annotation></semantics></math>: contrastive, <math id="S5.T2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>: rank distillation. The last row uses extended training (80 epochs, lr <math id="S5.T2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>).</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Several findings emerge. First, <span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">alignment is the strongest individual loss</span>: it alone achieves Recall@10 of 0.567, outperforming contrastive-only (0.498) by a large margin. This is expected, as alignment provides direct per-query supervision toward the teacher embedding. Second, <span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">rank distillation cannot stand alone</span>—it collapses completely without alignment to anchor the embedding space. However, when combined with alignment, it provides consistent gains. Third, <span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">all three losses together outperform any pair</span>, with the full combination reaching 0.595 Recall@10 under standard training. Finally, <span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">extended training with lower learning rate</span> provides the largest single improvement, pushing from 0.595 to 0.607.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>Training Recipe Analysis</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 5.3 Training Recipe Analysis ‣ 5 Results ‣ One Model Is Enough: Native Retrieval Embeddings from LLM Agent Hidden States" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> examines the effect of training duration and learning rate.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T3" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S5.T3.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S5.T3.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T3.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T3.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Epochs / LR</span></th>
<th id="S5.T3.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T3.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">R@10</span></th>
<th id="S5.T3.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T3.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MRR</span></th>
<th id="S5.T3.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T3.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Status</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S5.T3.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T3.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">30 / <math id="S5.T3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="S5.T3.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">0.595</td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">0.288</td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">converged</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T3.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T3.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">50 / <math id="S5.T3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="S5.T3.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.552</td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.261</td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">converged</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T3.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T3.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">80 / <math id="S5.T3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="S5.T3.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S5.T3.2.4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.607</span></td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S5.T3.2.4.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.293</span></td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">converged</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T3.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T3.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb">30 / <math id="S5.T3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="S5.T3.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">0.001</td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">0.000</td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">collapsed</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span>Effect of training epochs and learning rate. Too-high learning rate causes training collapse; longer training with lower learning rate yields the best results.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The results reveal that this projection task is sensitive to optimization dynamics. A learning rate of <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math> causes complete collapse, while the combination of 80 epochs with <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math> yields the best performance. Interestingly, 50 epochs at <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math> underperforms 30 epochs at the same rate, suggesting that longer training without reducing the learning rate can lead to overfitting or oscillation around the optimum.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.4 </span>Loss Weight Tuning</h3>

<div id="S5.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We also examine the sensitivity to rank distillation weight and top-<math id="S5.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> (Table <a href="#S5.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 5.4 Loss Weight Tuning ‣ 5 Results ‣ One Model Is Enough: Native Retrieval Embeddings from LLM Agent Hidden States" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T4" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S5.T4.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S5.T4.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T4.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T4.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Config</span></th>
<th id="S5.T4.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><math id="S5.T4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> / <math id="S5.T4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{r}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S5.T4.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><math id="S5.T4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S5.T4.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T4.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">R@10</span></th>
<th id="S5.T4.2.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T4.2.1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MRR</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S5.T4.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T4.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">Align-heavy</td>
<td id="S5.T4.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">1.0 / 0.5</td>
<td id="S5.T4.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">128</td>
<td id="S5.T4.2.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">0.591</td>
<td id="S5.T4.2.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">0.280</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T4.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T4.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">RD-heavy</td>
<td id="S5.T4.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.5 / 1.0</td>
<td id="S5.T4.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">128</td>
<td id="S5.T4.2.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.516</td>
<td id="S5.T4.2.3.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.236</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T4.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T4.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb">Top-256</td>
<td id="S5.T4.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">0.5 / 0.5</td>
<td id="S5.T4.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">256</td>
<td id="S5.T4.2.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">0.584</td>
<td id="S5.T4.2.4.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">0.284</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 4: </span>Rank distillation weight and top-<math id="S5.T4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> sensitivity. All use <math id="S5.T4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{c}=0.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{c}=0.0</annotation></semantics></math>, 30 epochs, lr <math id="S5.T4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Over-weighting rank distillation (<math id="S5.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{r}=1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{r}=1.0</annotation></semantics></math>) degrades performance substantially, while giving more weight to alignment (<math id="S5.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{a}=1.0,\lambda_{r}=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{a}=1.0,\lambda_{r}=0.5</annotation></semantics></math>) remains competitive. Increasing the candidate pool from <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>128</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=128</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=256</annotation></semantics></math> has minimal effect, suggesting that the ranking signal saturates beyond a moderate number of candidates.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Analysis</h2>

<section id="S6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.1 </span>Per-Conversation Agreement</h3>

<div id="S6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To understand where our method succeeds and fails, we analyze retrieval agreement at the conversation level. Across 346 conversations, the average per-conversation agreement rate between our method and the baseline is 84.5%. However, this average masks substantial variance: we identify 48 <em id="S6.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">failure-concentrated</em> conversations where our method’s recall is substantially below the baseline (with at least 3 triggers per conversation). This suggests that failures are not uniformly distributed but cluster around specific conversational patterns.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.2 </span>When Does Projection Fail?</h3>

<div id="S6.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The win/tie/loss breakdown (140/1843/206) reveals that our method loses on more triggers than it wins (206 vs. 140), though the majority are ties. We hypothesize that failure cases correspond to queries where the mapping from hidden states to embedding space is particularly difficult—for example, when the generated query involves rare terms, complex coreference chains, or domain-specific vocabulary that the projection head has not seen sufficient training examples for. The projection head must learn a cross-space mapping from the LLM’s causal hidden states to the embedding model’s bidirectional representations, and this mapping may be less reliable in the long tail of query types.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.3 </span>The Role of Training Duration</h3>

<div id="S6.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A striking finding is that extended training (80 epochs) with a lower learning rate provides the single largest improvement, contributing more than any individual loss component. This suggests that the projection task requires careful optimization: the mapping between LLM hidden space and embedding space involves subtle geometric relationships that benefit from slow, thorough convergence. The sensitivity to learning rate—with <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math> causing complete collapse while <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math> produces the best results—further supports this interpretation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.4 </span>Why Rank Distillation Cannot Stand Alone</h3>

<div id="S6.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Rank distillation alone collapses entirely (Recall@10 <math id="S6.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=0.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>0.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=0.001</annotation></semantics></math>), despite being a well-motivated objective. This is because rank distillation operates on <em id="S6.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">relative</em> document scores without anchoring the predicted embeddings to any absolute location in the embedding space. Without alignment loss to establish a reasonable initial embedding geometry, the rank distillation gradients provide no useful signal—the model cannot learn to rank documents correctly if its embeddings are not yet in a meaningful region of the space. However, once alignment establishes the geometric foundation, rank distillation provides complementary gains by refining the local ranking structure.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Limitations</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our work has several limitations. First, we evaluate on a single dataset (QReCC); generalization to other conversational search benchmarks or open-domain retrieval tasks remains to be demonstrated. Second, our experiments use a same-family setting (Qwen3-8B and Qwen3-Embedding-8B), which may benefit from shared pretraining representations. Cross-family settings (e.g., projecting from one LLM family into an unrelated embedding space) are likely more challenging and are left for future work. Third, while the quality gap is small (–3.0% to –3.6%), it is statistically significant (<math id="Sx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.0005" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.0005</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.0005</annotation></semantics></math>), meaning our method does not yet fully match the baseline. Fourth, the projection head requires training data generated by running both the LLM and the embedding model, so the embedding model is still needed during the training phase—only inference-time deployment is simplified. Finally, we have not explored whether the projection head can generalize to documents outside the training distribution without retraining.</p>
</div>
</section>
